  "query_preview": "..Convection has largely waned late this evening\nacross the area, with most of Utah and southwest Wyoming seeing \nsome relief from the rainfall during the overnight hours. Far\nsoutheastern Utah, largely near Lake Powell, may still see some\nshower and isolated thunderstorm development tonight and \nduring the morning hours as a weak shortwave trough passes across\nthe Four Corners. Uncertainty still remains with the timing of\nthis wave, which will ultimately determine the coverage/intensity\nof afternoon convection across far southeastern Utah. Earlier\nmodel runs suggested showers moving into the area by 9AM which\nwould likely result in more limited afternoon instability.\nHowever, recent model trends show this a bit later, coincident\nwith daytime heating/instability. \n\nAcross the rest of the area, widespread thunderstorms will develop\nduring the afternoon, owing to anomalous moisture in place \ncoupled with broad-scale dynamics. The aforementioned shortwave\ntrough moving through the Four Corners will move in sync with a\nseparate shortwave trough crossing western Utah from southwest to\nnortheast, providing plenty of lift and deep layer shear, \nespecially when considering upper-level diffluence already in \nplace. Thunderstorms will form as this shortwave lifts, first \nentering the St. George vicinity late morning and becoming \nwidespread by mid-afternoon as instability peaks. \n\nWhile outflow winds are also possible along the leading edge of\nthese storms (especially over west-central and northwest Utah), \nthe environment will be very favorable for flash flooding, \nnotably across southern and eastern Utah and over sensitive areas \nsuch as slot canyons and near recent burn scars. This flash flood\nthreat is especially high given recent rainfall across much of the\nstate. High-res guidance suggests storms will be capable of\nproducing rainfall rates potentially in excess of 1-2\"/hr, with\naverage return intervals (ARIs) exceeding 10-20 years across much\nof eastern and southern Utah...locally higher in places.\n\nConditions trend much drier after Monday, and even drier following\nthe passage of another shortwave trough mid-week. However, this\nbreak may be short-lived. Model guidance suggest moisture of\ntropical origins returns by the weekend, coupled with another\ndisturbance from the PacNW. Plenty of uncertainty still remains,\nbut this could yet again increase the flash flood threat heading\ninto next weekend.",
